# Insurance issuer reference

A local fictional member portal and clinic API. Insurer branding is illustrative and implies no partnership. No eligibility inquiry or billing write is sent.

```sh
bun install
bun run doctor
bun run dev
```

Open http://127.0.0.1:4320. Load Casey (policyholder, legacy response) or Jamie (dependent, newer response), then simulate a read. View coverage dates, copays, deductibles, out-of-pocket amounts, timestamps and complete JSON. Missing values remain **Not supplied**. The issuer update controls change the fictional copay, terminate enrollment and restore its seed. A revoked reference stays invalid; restored enrollment receives a new card on download.

## Connect an issuer

- Confirm `payerId` against the plan's actual routing and use issuer-authorized branding/signing.
- Replace `createEnrollmentLoader()` in `enrollment.ts` with an authoritative lookup. Active records require `status: "active"`, `credentialExpiresAt`, `member` and optional benefits. Termination may be only `{ "status": "terminated" }`. `parseEnrollment()` validates the contract. Preserve original benefit observation/expiration times; credential expiry is separate from coverage ending.
- Use the issuer's member session and member-specific clinic authorization. The default demo cookie and clinic header are fictional selectors.

For the JWT-based reference adapter, configure the actual authorities and identity/grant mappings in a private `auth.json` based on `auth.example.json`, then run `bun run pilot`. This mode exposes only the API, uses a separate database and admits no demo selectors or fixture updates. Trusted signatures, issuer/audience/expiration and member-specific clinic grants are required. Keep the server behind the host's HTTPS/application boundary. The issuer must supply its real identity, consent and clinic directories; the SDK does not create accounts or grants.

Add `/member/pass.pkpass` to the existing authenticated portal. Both read endpoints return full benefits and an unsent request. Connect verified enrollment events to `integration.sync(memberKey)` in the host's event handler; use `integration.revoke(memberKey)` for a compromised card reference. Neither method terminates an insurance policy.

## Configure Apple signing

Replace the sample Wallet pass/team identifiers in `issuer.json`. Add a `signing` object with `certificate`, `privateKey` and `wwdr` paths, plus optional `passphraseEnvironment`. Paths are relative to the config or absolute. Keep keys outside version control and restart after configuration changes.

The default project requests NFC and includes an encryption pair. It includes no Apple signing identity or NFC permission. `doctor` exits with status 1 until required setup passes; the software demo still runs. NFC requests never silently fall back to ordinary signing. For an explicit ordinary Wallet test, remove `wallet.encryptionPublicKey`; that card cannot tap. Keep `demo: true` for these fictional records.

See the canonical [onboarding guide](https://github.com/useTaiga/open-insurance/blob/main/docs/issuer-onboarding.md), [coverage model](https://github.com/useTaiga/open-insurance/blob/main/docs/intake.md) and [pilot workflow](https://github.com/useTaiga/open-insurance/blob/main/docs/pilot.md) for configuration, authenticated reader commands, verification and deployment responsibilities. Physical NFC, real identity/enrollment integration and production operation require separate validation. Apple push updates are not included.
